{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XGEFXoHrMyjsVGL6MwqMGD","type":"tool_result","content":"diff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\nindex b9d2c298..501566db 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n@@ -293,6 +293,10 @@ pose start spec:<slug> [--json|--apply --digest <sha256> [--json]|--status [--js\n pose adopt --list | <capability> [--off|--decline|--defer --reason <text>] [--date YYYY-MM-DD] [--apply] | --request <act-id> [--apply]  # decide a capability from the catalog\n pose setup [--json] [--no-input]  # what is in force, new and missing; the next step, confirmed one at a time\n pose identity add [<principal>] --key <file.pub> [--role <role>] [--apply] | list [--json] | remove <principal> [--fingerprint <fp>] [--apply]  # keys that prove answers\n+pose action <open|list|show|statement|resolve|cancel|waive|invalidate> ...  # open, read and resolve material requests to a person or an external system\n+pose issuer init <name> | pose issuer pin <name|name:public-key> [--attestations] [--human-authority] [--audience <id>] [--apply] | pose issuer list [--json]\n+                                   # native issuers that sign review attestations; keys live outside the project\n+pose migrate v7 --dry-run [--json]  # dry-run the legacy inventory a future major would convert, keep or refuse\n pose knowledge-housekeeping <list-expired|archive-expired|purge-archived> [--dry-run|--apply]\n pose knowledge-usage [--json]\n pose reports-housekeeping <list-stale|archive-stale|purge-archived> [--older-than N] [--dry-run|--apply]\n@@ -328,6 +332,7 @@ pose release <plan|prepare|check|notes|record|status|open-next|backfill> --versi\n                                    # prepare freezes manifest/notes; record imports\n                                    # provider evidence; status projects the state\n pose release-notes --version vX.Y.Z  # compatibility alias for the immutable notes\n+pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out <dir>  # package-manager manifests of a release\n ```\n \n ### Command reference\n@@ -339,7 +344,7 @@ pose release-notes --version vX.Y.Z  # compatibility alias for the immutable not\n - `spec-transfer` — explicit cross-project authority transfer. `preview` emits a deterministic digest-bound JSON plan without writing. Requirement mappings are explicit (`R1=equivalent:R1`, `R2=withdrawn`, `-=pending:R3`); apply requires the same digest plus one `--authorize-project` for every affected repository, and resume verifies the saved plan copies and receipts. Transfer is refused until both endpoints and every affected project adopt review-policy schema 4 (`qualified_artifact_refs_version: 1`, `spec_authority_transfer_version: 1`). Current dependency/membership links follow the verified redirect; the MCP tool `pose_spec_transfer_status` reads the journal only within its authorized project. `--mode reconcile-terminal` retires a coordinator spec onto an executor that is already `done` with a terminal closeout in its own project: the executor file is never written (apply only verifies its digest), the map is N:M over every source requirement and may target another spec with `destination_ref`, `withdrawn` needs a `rationale`, and `pending` needs a `rationale` plus an open target spec, recorded in the schema-2 redirect as `open_obligations`; `--map-file` reads the map as JSON. Engines without this mode refuse schema-2 plans and redirects. No automatic commits, pushes or policy adoption occur. Split-section specs without a single `spec.md` are reported as unsupported rather than rewritten.\n - `new-spec` — creates a new spec with standard lifecycle frontmatter and 7 engineering sections. By default writes the dated flat file `.pose/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md`; `--folder` writes `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>/spec.md` and `--legacy` writes `<slug>/spec.md`, all under [`.pose/specs/`](.pose/specs/).\n - `new-adr` — creates an ADR with the standard template and a deterministic slug.\n-- `new-roadmap` — creates a governed roadmap from [`.pose/templates/roadmap.md`](.pose/templates/roadmap.md): flat frontmatter (`status: draft|active|done|abandoned`, `depends_on:` between roadmaps) + milestones as `## Milestone: <id>` sections with flat bullets (`- after:`, `- target_start:`, `- target_due:`, `- specs:`). `check` validates single membership in active roadmaps, milestone/roadmap DAGs, dates and typed-ref resolution; `pose_spec_readiness` resolves those refs for real (milestone satisfied = its specs done; roadmap satisfied = status done). Dates are planning input; actuals derive from events.\n+- `new-roadmap` — creates a governed roadmap in `.pose/roadmaps/` from [`.pose/templates/roadmap.md`](.pose/templates/roadmap.md): flat frontmatter (`status: draft|active|done|abandoned`, `depends_on:` between roadmaps) + milestones as `## Milestone: <id>` sections with flat bullets (`- after:`, `- target_start:`, `- target_due:`, `- specs:`). `check` validates single membership in active roadmaps, milestone/roadmap DAGs, dates and typed-ref resolution; `pose_spec_readiness` resolves those refs for real (milestone satisfied = its specs done; roadmap satisfied = status done). Dates are planning input; actuals derive from events.\n - `new-knowledge` — creates an artifact in [`.pose/knowledge/`](.pose/knowledge/) from [`.pose/templates/knowledge.md`](.pose/templates/knowledge.md) with mandatory frontmatter (`type`, `owner`, `sensitivity`, `created_at`, `last_reviewed_at`, `expires_at`). Computes `expires_at` from the TTL (default 30d, max 90d).\n - `validate` — runs the declarative matrix in [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json): per-stack checks, per-module overrides, severity (`required`/`optional`) and mode (`strict`/`tolerant`). `--json-out`/`--junit`/`--sarif <path>` (`--json <path>` still works as a deprecated alias of `--json-out <path>`) emit the versioned structured result (schema 1) from one canonical model: stable check IDs (`<module>/<stack>/<name>`), command metadata, timing, severity, distinguishable outcomes (`pass|fail|error|skipped` — infra failures never masquerade as check failures), deterministic skip reasons, bounded captured output and secret redaction (configured env values only; inherited environment never enters the result). Text output stays authoritative; machine formats are additive. POSE-specific semantics survive the JUnit/SARIF projections via documented extensions (classname suffix / `pose/*` properties).\n   **Runtime guardrails:** every check runs under a timeout (`timeoutSeconds` per check, `defaults.timeoutSeconds`, safe default 600s) and an output ceiling (`defaults.maxOutputBytes`, default 1 MiB); breaching either terminates the process group and records the explicit state (`limit_state: timeout|output-limit`). Checks marked `isolation: \"required\"` never run locally — they are skipped with a machine-readable reason and exported by `--emit-plan <file>`: an execution-plan envelope binding project, spec, check plan, matrix digest, git HEAD and an approval slot to be stamped with an expiring execution identity before the Harness may run it.\n@@ -433,6 +438,14 @@ pose release-notes --version vX.Y.Z  # compatibility alias for the immutable not\n \n ### MCP connection contract\n \n+The `pose context --task <xref> --json` command and the `pose_mcp_context` tool\n+expose the current authority and revision without revealing paths. Cross-project\n+CLI writes require the target in `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` and the current\n+`context_revision` through `--expect-context`; discovering a project through\n+`HARNE8_PROJECTS_DIR` allows reading but does not grant write authorization.\n+For qualified creation, use `new-spec-qualified`: older engines refuse the\n+command before interpreting new options as ignored arguments.\n+\n - Treat `.mcp.json`, the connected server process and the selected project as\n   separate states.\n - Run `pose doctor --json` only for local static configuration; its\n@@ -481,6 +494,7 @@ distribution (`pose-action/`).\n - `moduleOverrides` adjusts stack, mode and extra checks per module\n   (`pose init --wizard` seeds them from repository scan).\n - `required` in a `strict` or `tolerant` module → exit 1; `optional` failures don't block.\n+- Logs use standardized `-> command` lines and a final summary, consumed by `pose report`.\n \n ---\n \n@@ -489,16 +503,24 @@ distribution (`pose-action/`).\n The full loop (create → consult in workflows → schema validation → CI gate →\n housekeeping) is available from installation; maturity comes from usage.\n \n-Write path: `pose new-knowledge <type> <slug>`.\n-Read path: the feature/bugfix/review workflows include \"consult\n-`.pose/knowledge/`\" as a mandatory checklist step.\n-Gate: `pose knowledge-check --strict`, used in CI.\n+Write path: `pose new-knowledge <type> <slug>` generates an artifact from [`.pose/templates/knowledge.md`](.pose/templates/knowledge.md) with validated frontmatter.\n+\n+Read path: the [feature](.pose/workflows/feature.md), [bugfix](.pose/workflows/bugfix.md) and [review](.pose/workflows/review.md) workflows include \"consult `.pose/knowledge/`\" as a mandatory checklist step.\n+\n+Gate: `pose knowledge-check --strict` validates the schema natively together with the overdue backlog; used in CI.\n+\n+Criteria for considering the subsystem continuously \"healthy\":\n+\n+- a dedicated knowledge-governance spec (create it with `pose new-spec` when activating the subsystem);\n+- a dedicated rule, [`knowledge-governance.md`](.pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md);\n+- defined ownership (e.g. `@pose-maintainers`) with biweekly/monthly review;\n+- minimal housekeeping through `pose knowledge-housekeeping`.\n \n-Health criteria: a dedicated governance spec when activating the subsystem;\n-the [`knowledge-governance.md`](.pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md) rule;\n-defined ownership with biweekly/monthly review; minimal housekeeping.\n On repeated neglect (overdue items untreated for 2 cycles), treat `knowledge`\n as degraded and block functional expansion until it recovers.\n+The transition from \"healthy\" to \"mature\" requires two consecutive cycles with\n+`pose knowledge-check --strict` passing and at least one documented consultation\n+per feature in active specs.\n \n ---\n \n@@ -806,7 +828,7 @@ Amend and close share the per-spec lifecycle lock. The append writes baseline th\n \n ### Terminal closeout verification\n \n-Lifecycle `done` records the transition; terminal verification reads the applicable review contract and current opted-in amendment/action/federation gates. `pose closeout-check`, `pose close --plan` and MCP `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` share that verifier. A done plan exposes `lifecycle_done`, `verification` with `verification_basis` and `limitations`, and `next_action`; historical pipeline steps are `not-run`, not inferred as executed. `verify` reports the current query's verdict. `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` also returns a typed `handoff` when the plan has steps left and nothing blocks it: the `pose close --apply --digest <full plan digest>` invocation as structured arguments, the project and root the caller selected, the digests it was computed from, the authority it needs and when it stops being valid; it executes nothing and is not an approval (no handoff is returned for a plan that is terminal, blocked or on an unknown read view). Every MCP tool has a declared route class (domain-direct, cli-wrapper, read-only-planning, admitted-write, external-handoff), and `pose_suggest` and `pose_get_followups` run the CLI command in process unless `POSE_EXECUTABLE` is set or the installed binary was replaced since the server started, in which case they are spawned. Existing review cutoffs and sealed historical contracts remain applicable; a legacy exemption is disclosed without inventing an execution receipt.\n+Lifecycle `done` records the transition; terminal verification reads the applicable review contract and current opted-in amendment/action/federation gates. `pose closeout-check`, `pose close --plan` and MCP `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` share that verifier. A done plan exposes `lifecycle_done`, `verification` with `verification_basis` and `limitations`, and `next_action`; historical pipeline steps are `not-run`, not inferred as executed. `verify` reports the current query's verdict. `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` also returns a typed `handoff` when the plan has steps left and nothing blocks it: the `pose close --apply --digest <full plan digest>` invocation as structured arguments, the arguments the performer still owes (`required_inputs`, the `--reviewer` identity when a mechanical attestation is pending), the project the caller selected (by its logical id and the digest of its root, never a path), the digests it was computed from, the authority it needs and when it stops being valid; it executes nothing and is not an approval (no handoff is returned for a plan that is terminal, blocked or on an unknown read view). Every MCP tool has a declared route class (domain-direct, cli-wrapper, read-only-planning, admitted-write, external-handoff), and `pose_suggest` and `pose_get_followups` run the CLI command in process unless `POSE_EXECUTABLE` is set or the installed binary was replaced since the server started, in which case they are spawned. Existing review cutoffs and sealed historical contracts remain applicable; a legacy exemption is disclosed without inventing an execution receipt.\n \n For a done scope, `pose close --apply|--resume` is readonly: verified state returns `already-satisfied` with empty `steps_run` and `effects`, while missing required proof or invalid history returns blocked with a supported repair action. Neither outcome rewrites checkpoints, starts, amendments or lifecycle. Plain `pose close` also verifies instead of repeating the transition. An in-progress plan checks the existing amendment gate before sealing. No policy, review or historical receipt is created automatically.\n \ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex c7830d49..0ce99ad0 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -297,6 +297,10 @@ pose start spec:<slug> [--json|--apply --digest <sha256> [--json]|--status [--js\n pose adopt --list | <capability> [--off|--decline|--defer --reason <texto>] [--date YYYY-MM-DD] [--apply] | --request <act-id> [--apply]  # decide uma capacidade do catálogo\n pose setup [--json] [--no-input]  # o que está em vigor, novo e faltando; o próximo passo, confirmado um a um\n pose identity add [<principal>] --key <file.pub> [--role <papel>] [--apply] | list [--json] | remove <principal> [--fingerprint <fp>] [--apply]  # chaves que provam respostas\n+pose action <open|list|show|statement|resolve|cancel|waive|invalidate> ...  # abre, lê e resolve pedidos materiais a uma pessoa ou a um sistema externo\n+pose issuer init <nome> | pose issuer pin <nome|nome:chave-pública> [--attestations] [--human-authority] [--audience <id>] [--apply] | pose issuer list [--json]\n+                                   # issuers nativos que assinam atestações de review; as chaves ficam fora do projeto\n+pose migrate v7 --dry-run [--json]  # simula o inventário legado que um major futuro converteria, manteria ou recusaria\n pose knowledge-housekeeping <list-expired|archive-expired|purge-archived> [--dry-run|--apply]\n pose knowledge-usage [--json]\n pose reports-housekeeping <list-stale|archive-stale|purge-archived> [--older-than N] [--dry-run|--apply]\n@@ -332,6 +336,7 @@ pose release <plan|prepare|check|notes|record|status|open-next|backfill> --versi\n                                    # prepare congela manifest/notes; record importa\n                                    # evidência do provider; status projeta o estado\n pose release-notes --version vX.Y.Z # alias de compatibilidade para as notes imutáveis\n+pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out <dir>  # manifestos de gerenciadores de pacote de uma release\n ```\n \n ### Referência de comandos\n@@ -423,6 +428,8 @@ pose release-notes --version vX.Y.Z # alias de compatibilidade para as notes imu\n - `knowledge-suggest <query>` / `semantic-suggest <query>` / `suggest-feedback` / `portfolio-projection` / `reconcile-evidence` — ranqueamento léxico determinístico e projeções consultivas para conhecimento, agrupamento de feedback, marcos de portfólio multi-roadmap e reconciliação de evidências de validação. Sugestões nunca bloqueiam nem se autoaplicam sem confirmação.\n - `hooks` — gerencia symlinks do binário nativo em `.git/hooks/`. O nome de invocação seleciona `check --tolerant` para `pre-commit` e `index` para `post-merge`; `install --force` preserva backup de hooks preexistentes.\n - `version` — exibe versão compilada do binário Go, SHA do commit e compara a versão do schema do repositório com os requisitos do motor.\n+- `context` — resolve um projeto lógico selecionado e uma tarefa opcional, local ou qualificada por `xref:`, pelo resolvedor compartilhado de artefatos. O resultado JSON traz a autoridade canônica, as revisões do projeto e do artefato, o estado de resolução, os contratos suportados e um `context_revision`; nunca inclui roots do filesystem. Use esse token para detectar checkout ou revisão alterados antes de uma escrita entre projetos.\n+- Escritas CLI entre projetos exigem o destino em `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` e o `context_revision` de uma chamada recente de `pose context`. Use `new-spec-qualified` para criação qualificada, de modo que motores antigos recusem o próprio comando. Um projeto encontrado apenas por varredura de `HARNE8_PROJECTS_DIR` pode ser lido, mas não concede acesso de escrita. Specs canônicas existentes são reutilizadas; redirecionamentos e transferências incompletas bloqueiam a criação de uma cópia.\n - `install <dir> [--locale tag] [--skip-mcp] [--force] [--no-backup] [--allow-non-git]` — instala o runtime, regras, workflows, templates e documentação embutidos do POSE em um diretório de destino sem clonar, grava no `adopted_at` da política de changelog o dia em que a instância a recebeu e roda o gate estrito.\n - `import <spec-kit|openspec> <path> [--dry-run]` — importa especificações externas para o formato canônico do POSE com frontmatter e 7 seções padronizadas.\n - `serve-mcp` — inicia o servidor POSE Model Context Protocol via stdio (`--stdio`) ou HTTP para integração com agentes de IA. O servidor stdio encerra com SIGTERM; ele resolve o CLI `pose` que executa uma única vez, ao iniciar, então um servidor iniciado antes de um `pose update` continua funcionando com o binário atualizado.\n@@ -487,7 +494,8 @@ POSE (`pose-action/`).\n \n - Fonte única: [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json).\n - Stacks base: `node`, `go`, `rust`, `java` (Maven/Gradle).\n-- `moduleOverrides` ajusta stack, modo e checks adicionais por módulo.\n+- `moduleOverrides` ajusta stack, modo e checks adicionais por módulo\n+  (`pose init --wizard` os semeia a partir da varredura do repositório).\n - `required` em módulo `strict` ou `tolerant` → exit 1; `optional` falha não bloqueia pipeline.\n - Logs padronizados com linhas `-> comando` e resumo final para consumo por `pose report`.\n \n@@ -829,7 +837,7 @@ Amend e close compartilham o lifecycle lock por spec. O append grava baseline e\n \n ### Verificação terminal de closeout\n \n-Lifecycle `done` registra a transição; a verificação terminal lê o contrato de review aplicável e os gates atuais opt-in de amendments/ações/federação. `pose closeout-check`, `pose close --plan` e MCP `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` compartilham esse verificador. O plano done expõe `lifecycle_done`, `verification` com `verification_basis` e `limitations`, e `next_action`; etapas históricas do pipeline ficam `not-run`, sem inferir execução. `verify` informa o veredito da consulta atual. `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` também devolve um `handoff` tipado quando o plano tem etapas restantes e nada o bloqueia: a invocação `pose close --apply --digest <digest completo do plano>` como argumentos estruturados, o projeto e a raiz que o chamador selecionou, os digests a partir dos quais foi calculado, a autoridade necessária e quando deixa de valer; não executa nada e não é uma aprovação (plano terminal, bloqueado ou com read view desconhecido não recebe handoff). Toda ferramenta MCP tem uma classe de rota declarada (domain-direct, cli-wrapper, read-only-planning, admitted-write, external-handoff), e `pose_suggest` e `pose_get_followups` rodam o comando da CLI em processo, a menos que `POSE_EXECUTABLE` esteja definido ou o binário instalado tenha sido substituído desde que o servidor iniciou, caso em que são executados em processo separado. Cutoffs existentes e contratos históricos selados continuam aplicáveis; a isenção legada é divulgada sem inventar um recibo de execução.\n+Lifecycle `done` registra a transição; a verificação terminal lê o contrato de review aplicável e os gates atuais opt-in de amendments/ações/federação. `pose closeout-check`, `pose close --plan` e MCP `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` compartilham esse verificador. O plano done expõe `lifecycle_done`, `verification` com `verification_basis` e `limitations`, e `next_action`; etapas históricas do pipeline ficam `not-run`, sem inferir execução. `verify` informa o veredito da consulta atual. `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` também devolve um `handoff` tipado quando o plano tem etapas restantes e nada o bloqueia: a invocação `pose close --apply --digest <digest completo do plano>` como argumentos estruturados, os argumentos que o executor ainda deve (`required_inputs`, a identidade `--reviewer` quando há atestação mecânica pendente), o projeto que o chamador selecionou (pelo id lógico e pelo digest da raiz, nunca por um caminho), os digests a partir dos quais foi calculado, a autoridade necessária e quando deixa de valer; não executa nada e não é uma aprovação (plano terminal, bloqueado ou com read view desconhecido não recebe handoff). Toda ferramenta MCP tem uma classe de rota declarada (domain-direct, cli-wrapper, read-only-planning, admitted-write, external-handoff), e `pose_suggest` e `pose_get_followups` rodam o comando da CLI em processo, a menos que `POSE_EXECUTABLE` esteja definido ou o binário instalado tenha sido substituído desde que o servidor iniciou, caso em que são executados em processo separado. Cutoffs existentes e contratos históricos selados continuam aplicáveis; a isenção legada é divulgada sem inventar um recibo de execução.\n \n Para um scope done, `pose close --apply|--resume` é readonly: estado verificado retorna `already-satisfied`, com `steps_run` e `effects` vazios; prova obrigatória ausente ou histórico inválido retorna blocked com ação de reparo suportada. Nenhum resultado reescreve checkpoints, starts, amendments ou lifecycle. `pose close` simples também verifica em vez de repetir a transição. Um plano in-progress verifica o gate existente de amendments antes de selar. Nenhuma policy, review ou recibo histórico é criado automaticamente.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"2afa2ce5-6b2d-4e48-8469-27421d0650a4","timestamp":"2026-11-09T07:41:16.926Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"diff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\nindex b9d2c298..501566db 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n@@ -293,6 +293,10 @@ pose start spec:<slug> [--json|--apply --digest <sha256> [--json]|--status [--js\n pose adopt --list | <capability> [--off|--decline|--defer --reason <text>] [--date YYYY-MM-DD] [--apply] | --request <act-id> [--apply]  # decide a capability from the catalog\n pose setup [--json] [--no-input]  # what is in force, new and missing; the next step, confirmed one at a time\n pose identity add [<principal>] --key <file.pub> [--role <role>] [--apply] | list [--json] | remove <principal> [--fingerprint <fp>] [--apply]  # keys that prove answers\n+pose action <open|list|show|statement|resolve|cancel|waive|invalidate> ...  # open, read and resolve material requests to a person or an external system\n+pose issuer init <name> | pose issuer pin <name|name:public-key> [--attestations] [--human-authority] [--audience <id>] [--apply] | pose issuer list [--json]\n+                                   # native issuers that sign review attestations; keys live outside the project\n+pose migrate v7 --dry-run [--json]  # dry-run the legacy inventory a future major would convert, keep or refuse\n pose knowledge-housekeeping <list-expired|archive-expired|purge-archived> [--dry-run|--apply]\n pose knowledge-usage [--json]\n pose reports-housekeeping <list-stale|archive-stale|purge-archived> [--older-than N] [--dry-run|--apply]\n@@ -328,6 +332,7 @@ pose release <plan|prepare|check|notes|record|status|open-next|backfill> --versi\n                                    # prepare freezes manifest/notes; record imports\n                                    # provider evidence; status projects the state\n pose release-notes --version vX.Y.Z  # compatibility alias for the immutable notes\n+pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out <dir>  # package-manager manifests of a release\n ```\n \n ### Command reference\n@@ -339,7 +344,7 @@ pose release-notes --version vX.Y.Z  # compatibility alias for the immutable not\n - `spec-transfer` — explicit cross-project authority transfer. `preview` emits a deterministic digest-bound JSON plan without writing. Requirement mappings are explicit (`R1=equivalent:R1`, `R2=withdrawn`, `-=pending:R3`); apply requires the same digest plus one `--authorize-project` for every affected repository, and resume verifies the saved plan copies and receipts. Transfer is refused until both endpoints and every affected project adopt review-policy schema 4 (`qualified_artifact_refs_version: 1`, `spec_authority_transfer_version: 1`). Current dependency/membership links follow the verified redirect; the MCP tool `pose_spec_transfer_status` reads the journal only within its authorized project. `--mode reconcile-terminal` retires a coordinator spec onto an executor that is already `done` with a terminal closeout in its own project: the executor file is never written (apply only verifies its digest), the map is N:M over every source requirement and may target another spec with `destination_ref`, `withdrawn` needs a `rationale`, and `pending` needs a `rationale` plus an open target spec, recorded in the schema-2 redirect as `open_obligations`; `--map-file` reads the map as JSON. Engines without this mode refuse schema-2 plans and redirects. No automatic commits, pushes or policy adoption occur. Split-section specs without a single `spec.md` are reported as unsupported rather than rewritten.\n - `new-spec` — creates a new spec with standard lifecycle frontmatter and 7 engineering sections. By default writes the dated flat file `.pose/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md`; `--folder` writes `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>/spec.md` and `--legacy` writes `<slug>/spec.md`, all under [`.pose/specs/`](.pose/specs/).\n - `new-adr` — creates an ADR with the standard template and a deterministic slug.\n-- `new-roadmap` — creates a governed roadmap from [`.pose/templates/roadmap.md`](.pose/templates/roadmap.md): flat frontmatter (`status: draft|active|done|abandoned`, `depends_on:` between roadmaps) + milestones as `## Milestone: <id>` sections with flat bullets (`- after:`, `- target_start:`, `- target_due:`, `- specs:`). `check` validates single membership in active roadmaps, milestone/roadmap DAGs, dates and typed-ref resolution; `pose_spec_readiness` resolves those refs for real (milestone satisfied = its specs done; roadmap satisfied = status done). Dates are planning input; actuals derive from events.\n+- `new-roadmap` — creates a governed roadmap in `.pose/roadmaps/` from [`.pose/templates/roadmap.md`](.pose/templates/roadmap.md): flat frontmatter (`status: draft|active|done|abandoned`, `depends_on:` between roadmaps) + milestones as `## Milestone: <id>` sections with flat bullets (`- after:`, `- target_start:`, `- target_due:`, `- specs:`). `check` validates single membership in active roadmaps, milestone/roadmap DAGs, dates and typed-ref resolution; `pose_spec_readiness` resolves those refs for real (milestone satisfied = its specs done; roadmap satisfied = status done). Dates are planning input; actuals derive from events.\n - `new-knowledge` — creates an artifact in [`.pose/knowledge/`](.pose/knowledge/) from [`.pose/templates/knowledge.md`](.pose/templates/knowledge.md) with mandatory frontmatter (`type`, `owner`, `sensitivity`, `created_at`, `last_reviewed_at`, `expires_at`). Computes `expires_at` from the TTL (default 30d, max 90d).\n - `validate` — runs the declarative matrix in [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json): per-stack checks, per-module overrides, severity (`required`/`optional`) and mode (`strict`/`tolerant`). `--json-out`/`--junit`/`--sarif <path>` (`--json <path>` still works as a deprecated alias of `--json-out <path>`) emit the versioned structured result (schema 1) from one canonical model: stable check IDs (`<module>/<stack>/<name>`), command metadata, timing, severity, distinguishable outcomes (`pass|fail|error|skipped` — infra failures never masquerade as check failures), deterministic skip reasons, bounded captured output and secret redaction (configured env values only; inherited environment never enters the result). Text output stays authoritative; machine formats are additive. POSE-specific semantics survive the JUnit/SARIF projections via documented extensions (classname suffix / `pose/*` properties).\n   **Runtime guardrails:** every check runs under a timeout (`timeoutSeconds` per check, `defaults.timeoutSeconds`, safe default 600s) and an output ceiling (`defaults.maxOutputBytes`, default 1 MiB); breaching either terminates the process group and records the explicit state (`limit_state: timeout|output-limit`). Checks marked `isolation: \"required\"` never run locally — they are skipped with a machine-readable reason and exported by `--emit-plan <file>`: an execution-plan envelope binding project, spec, check plan, matrix digest, git HEAD and an approval slot to be stamped with an expiring execution identity before the Harness may run it.\n@@ -433,6 +438,14 @@ pose release-notes --version vX.Y.Z  # compatibility alias for the immutable not\n \n ### MCP connection contract\n \n+The `pose context --task <xref> --json` command and the `pose_mcp_context` tool\n+expose the current authority and revision without revealing paths. Cross-project\n+CLI writes require the target in `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` and the current\n+`context_revision` through `--expect-context`; discovering a project through\n+`HARNE8_PROJECTS_DIR` allows reading but does not grant write authorization.\n+For qualified creation, use `new-spec-qualified`: older engines refuse the\n+command before interpreting new options as ignored arguments.\n+\n - Treat `.mcp.json`, the connected server process and the selected project as\n   separate states.\n - Run `pose doctor --json` only for local static configuration; its\n@@ -481,6 +494,7 @@ distribution (`pose-action/`).\n - `moduleOverrides` adjusts stack, mode and extra checks per module\n   (`pose init --wizard` seeds them from repository scan).\n - `required` in a `strict` or `tolerant` module → exit 1; `optional` failures don't block.\n+- Logs use standardized `-> command` lines and a final summary, consumed by `pose report`.\n \n ---\n \n@@ -489,16 +503,24 @@ distribution (`pose-action/`).\n The full loop (create → consult in workflows → schema validation → CI gate →\n housekeeping) is available from installation; maturity comes from usage.\n \n-Write path: `pose new-knowledge <type> <slug>`.\n-Read path: the feature/bugfix/review workflows include \"consult\n-`.pose/knowledge/`\" as a mandatory checklist step.\n-Gate: `pose knowledge-check --strict`, used in CI.\n+Write path: `pose new-knowledge <type> <slug>` generates an artifact from [`.pose/templates/knowledge.md`](.pose/templates/knowledge.md) with validated frontmatter.\n+\n+Read path: the [feature](.pose/workflows/feature.md), [bugfix](.pose/workflows/bugfix.md) and [review](.pose/workflows/review.md) workflows include \"consult `.pose/knowledge/`\" as a mandatory checklist step.\n+\n+Gate: `pose knowledge-check --strict` validates the schema natively together with the overdue backlog; used in CI.\n+\n+Criteria for considering the subsystem continuously \"healthy\":\n+\n+- a dedicated knowledge-governance spec (create it with `pose new-spec` when activating the subsystem);\n+- a dedicated rule, [`knowledge-governance.md`](.pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md);\n+- defined ownership (e.g. `@pose-maintainers`) with biweekly/monthly review;\n+- minimal housekeeping through `pose knowledge-housekeeping`.\n \n-Health criteria: a dedicated governance spec when activating the subsystem;\n-the [`knowledge-governance.md`](.pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md) rule;\n-defined ownership with biweekly/monthly review; minimal housekeeping.\n On repeated neglect (overdue items untreated for 2 cycles), treat `knowledge`\n as degraded and block functional expansion until it recovers.\n+The transition from \"healthy\" to \"mature\" requires two consecutive cycles with\n+`pose knowledge-check --strict` passing and at least one documented consultation\n+per feature in active specs.\n \n ---\n \n@@ -806,7 +828,7 @@ Amend and close share the per-spec lifecycle lock. The append writes baseline th\n \n ### Terminal closeout verification\n \n-Lifecycle `done` records the transition; terminal verification reads the applicable review contract and current opted-in amendment/action/federation gates. `pose closeout-check`, `pose close --plan` and MCP `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` share that verifier. A done plan exposes `lifecycle_done`, `verification` with `verification_basis` and `limitations`, and `next_action`; historical pipeline steps are `not-run`, not inferred as executed. `verify` reports the current query's verdict. `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` also returns a typed `handoff` when the plan has steps left and nothing blocks it: the `pose close --apply --digest <full plan digest>` invocation as structured arguments, the project and root the caller selected, the digests it was computed from, the authority it needs and when it stops being valid; it executes nothing and is not an approval (no handoff is returned for a plan that is terminal, blocked or on an unknown read view). Every MCP tool has a declared route class (domain-direct, cli-wrapper, read-only-planning, admitted-write, external-handoff), and `pose_suggest` and `pose_get_followups` run the CLI command in process unless `POSE_EXECUTABLE` is set or the installed binary was replaced since the server started, in which case they are spawned. Existing review cutoffs and sealed historical contracts remain applicable; a legacy exemption is disclosed without inventing an execution receipt.\n+Lifecycle `done` records the transition; terminal verification reads the applicable review contract and current opted-in amendment/action/federation gates. `pose closeout-check`, `pose close --plan` and MCP `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` share that verifier. A done plan exposes `lifecycle_done`, `verification` with `verification_basis` and `limitations`, and `next_action`; historical pipeline steps are `not-run`, not inferred as executed. `verify` reports the current query's verdict. `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` also returns a typed `handoff` when the plan has steps left and nothing blocks it: the `pose close --apply --digest <full plan digest>` invocation as structured arguments, the arguments the performer still owes (`required_inputs`, the `--reviewer` identity when a mechanical attestation is pending), the project the caller selected (by its logical id and the digest of its root, never a path), the digests it was computed from, the authority it needs and when it stops being valid; it executes nothing and is not an approval (no handoff is returned for a plan that is terminal, blocked or on an unknown read view). Every MCP tool has a declared route class (domain-direct, cli-wrapper, read-only-planning, admitted-write, external-handoff), and `pose_suggest` and `pose_get_followups` run the CLI command in process unless `POSE_EXECUTABLE` is set or the installed binary was replaced since the server started, in which case they are spawned. Existing review cutoffs and sealed historical contracts remain applicable; a legacy exemption is disclosed without inventing an execution receipt.\n \n For a done scope, `pose close --apply|--resume` is readonly: verified state returns `already-satisfied` with empty `steps_run` and `effects`, while missing required proof or invalid history returns blocked with a supported repair action. Neither outcome rewrites checkpoints, starts, amendments or lifecycle. Plain `pose close` also verifies instead of repeating the transition. An in-progress plan checks the existing amendment gate before sealing. No policy, review or historical receipt is created automatically.\n \ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex c7830d49..0ce99ad0 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -297,6 +297,10 @@ pose start spec:<slug> [--json|--apply --digest <sha256> [--json]|--status [--js\n pose adopt --list | <capability> [--off|--decline|--defer --reason <texto>] [--date YYYY-MM-DD] [--apply] | --request <act-id> [--apply]  # decide uma capacidade do catálogo\n pose setup [--json] [--no-input]  # o que está em vigor, novo e faltando; o próximo passo, confirmado um a um\n pose identity add [<principal>] --key <file.pub> [--role <papel>] [--apply] | list [--json] | remove <principal> [--fingerprint <fp>] [--apply]  # chaves que provam respostas\n+pose action <open|list|show|statement|resolve|cancel|waive|invalidate> ...  # abre, lê e resolve pedidos materiais a uma pessoa ou a um sistema externo\n+pose issuer init <nome> | pose issuer pin <nome|nome:chave-pública> [--attestations] [--human-authority] [--audience <id>] [--apply] | pose issuer list [--json]\n+                                   # issuers nativos que assinam atestações de review; as chaves ficam fora do projeto\n+pose migrate v7 --dry-run [--json]  # simula o inventário legado que um major futuro converteria, manteria ou recusaria\n pose knowledge-housekeeping <list-expired|archive-expired|purge-archived> [--dry-run|--apply]\n pose knowledge-usage [--json]\n pose reports-housekeeping <list-stale|archive-stale|purge-archived> [--older-than N] [--dry-run|--apply]\n@@ -332,6 +336,7 @@ pose release <plan|prepare|check|notes|record|status|open-next|backfill> --versi\n                                    # prepare congela manifest/notes; record importa\n                                    # evidência do provider; status projeta o estado\n pose release-notes --version vX.Y.Z # alias de compatibilidade para as notes imutáveis\n+pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out <dir>  # manifestos de gerenciadores de pacote de uma release\n ```\n \n ### Referência de comandos\n@@ -423,6 +428,8 @@ pose release-notes --version vX.Y.Z # alias de compatibilidade para as notes imu\n - `knowledge-suggest <query>` / `semantic-suggest <query>` / `suggest-feedback` / `portfolio-projection` / `reconcile-evidence` — ranqueamento léxico determinístico e projeções consultivas para conhecimento, agrupamento de feedback, marcos de portfólio multi-roadmap e reconciliação de evidências de validação. Sugestões nunca bloqueiam nem se autoaplicam sem confirmação.\n - `hooks` — gerencia symlinks do binário nativo em `.git/hooks/`. O nome de invocação seleciona `check --tolerant` para `pre-commit` e `index` para `post-merge`; `install --force` preserva backup de hooks preexistentes.\n - `version` — exibe versão compilada do binário Go, SHA do commit e compara a versão do schema do repositório com os requisitos do motor.\n+- `context` — resolve um projeto lógico selecionado e uma tarefa opcional, local ou qualificada por `xref:`, pelo resolvedor compartilhado de artefatos. O resultado JSON traz a autoridade canônica, as revisões do projeto e do artefato, o estado de resolução, os contratos suportados e um `context_revision`; nunca inclui roots do filesystem. Use esse token para detectar checkout ou revisão alterados antes de uma escrita entre projetos.\n+- Escritas CLI entre projetos exigem o destino em `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` e o `context_revision` de uma chamada recente de `pose context`. Use `new-spec-qualified` para criação qualificada, de modo que motores antigos recusem o próprio comando. Um projeto encontrado apenas por varredura de `HARNE8_PROJECTS_DIR` pode ser lido, mas não concede acesso de escrita. Specs canônicas existentes são reutilizadas; redirecionamentos e transferências incompletas bloqueiam a criação de uma cópia.\n - `install <dir> [--locale tag] [--skip-mcp] [--force] [--no-backup] [--allow-non-git]` — instala o runtime, regras, workflows, templates e documentação embutidos do POSE em um diretório de destino sem clonar, grava no `adopted_at` da política de changelog o dia em que a instância a recebeu e roda o gate estrito.\n - `import <spec-kit|openspec> <path> [--dry-run]` — importa especificações externas para o formato canônico do POSE com frontmatter e 7 seções padronizadas.\n - `serve-mcp` — inicia o servidor POSE Model Context Protocol via stdio (`--stdio`) ou HTTP para integração com agentes de IA. O servidor stdio encerra com SIGTERM; ele resolve o CLI `pose` que executa uma única vez, ao iniciar, então um servidor iniciado antes de um `pose update` continua funcionando com o binário atualizado.\n@@ -487,7 +494,8 @@ POSE (`pose-action/`).\n \n - Fonte única: [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json).\n - Stacks base: `node`, `go`, `rust`, `java` (Maven/Gradle).\n-- `moduleOverrides` ajusta stack, modo e checks adicionais por módulo.\n+- `moduleOverrides` ajusta stack, modo e checks adicionais por módulo\n+  (`pose init --wizard` os semeia a partir da varredura do repositório).\n - `required` em módulo `strict` ou `tolerant` → exit 1; `optional` falha não bloqueia pipeline.\n - Logs padronizados com linhas `-> comando` e resumo final para consumo por `pose report`.\n \n@@ -829,7 +837,7 @@ Amend e close compartilham o lifecycle lock por spec. O append grava baseline e\n \n ### Verificação terminal de closeout\n \n-Lifecycle `done` registra a transição; a verificação terminal lê o contrato de review aplicável e os gates atuais opt-in de amendments/ações/federação. `pose closeout-check`, `pose close --plan` e MCP `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` compartilham esse verificador. O plano done expõe `lifecycle_done`, `verification` com `verification_basis` e `limitations`, e `next_action`; etapas históricas do pipeline ficam `not-run`, sem inferir execução. `verify` informa o veredito da consulta atual. `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` também devolve um `handoff` tipado quando o plano tem etapas restantes e nada o bloqueia: a invocação `pose close --apply --digest <digest completo do plano>` como argumentos estruturados, o projeto e a raiz que o chamador selecionou, os digests a partir dos quais foi calculado, a autoridade necessária e quando deixa de valer; não executa nada e não é uma aprovação (plano terminal, bloqueado ou com read view desconhecido não recebe handoff). Toda ferramenta MCP tem uma classe de rota declarada (domain-direct, cli-wrapper, read-only-planning, admitted-write, external-handoff), e `pose_suggest` e `pose_get_followups` rodam o comando da CLI em processo, a menos que `POSE_EXECUTABLE` esteja definido ou o binário instalado tenha sido substituído desde que o servidor iniciou, caso em que são executados em processo separado. Cutoffs existentes e contratos históricos selados continuam aplicáveis; a isenção legada é divulgada sem inventar um recibo de execução.\n+Lifecycle `done` registra a transição; a verificação terminal lê o contrato de review aplicável e os gates atuais opt-in de amendments/ações/federação. `pose closeout-check`, `pose close --plan` e MCP `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` compartilham esse verificador. O plano done expõe `lifecycle_done`, `verification` com `verification_basis` e `limitations`, e `next_action`; etapas históricas do pipeline ficam `not-run`, sem inferir execução. `verify` informa o veredito da consulta atual. `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` também devolve um `handoff` tipado quando o plano tem etapas restantes e nada o bloqueia: a invocação `pose close --apply --digest <digest completo do plano>` como argumentos estruturados, os argumentos que o executor ainda deve (`required_inputs`, a identidade `--reviewer` quando há atestação mecânica pendente), o projeto que o chamador selecionou (pelo id lógico e pelo digest da raiz, nunca por um caminho), os digests a partir dos quais foi calculado, a autoridade necessária e quando deixa de valer; não executa nada e não é uma aprovação (plano terminal, bloqueado ou com read view desconhecido não recebe handoff). Toda ferramenta MCP tem uma classe de rota declarada (domain-direct, cli-wrapper, read-only-planning, admitted-write, external-handoff), e `pose_suggest` e `pose_get_followups` rodam o comando da CLI em processo, a menos que `POSE_EXECUTABLE` esteja definido ou o binário instalado tenha sido substituído desde que o servidor iniciou, caso em que são executados em processo separado. Cutoffs existentes e contratos históricos selados continuam aplicáveis; a isenção legada é divulgada sem inventar um recibo de execução.\n \n Para um scope done, `pose close --apply|--resume` é readonly: estado verificado retorna `already-satisfied`, com `steps_run` e `effects` vazios; prova obrigatória ausente ou histórico inválido retorna blocked com ação de reparo suportada. Nenhum resultado reescreve checkpoints, starts, amendments ou lifecycle. `pose close` simples também verifica em vez de repetir a transição. Um plano in-progress verifica o gate existente de amendments antes de selar. Nenhuma policy, review ou recibo histórico é criado automaticamente.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XGEFXoHrMyjsVGL6MwqMGD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"subcommandResults"}}]}
